# ============================ ACCOUNTS 7 ============================
LESSONS_A7 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The period close", "11", "<p>The period close is the monthly procedure that takes a month from trading to sealed. This module covers the close itself, the cut-off discipline at its boundary, the three financial statements produced from a sealed month, the fixed-asset layer the statements require, and the year-end.</p><p><b>Definition.</b> A close is complete when:</p><p>1. All the month's documents are in (cut-off — Chapter 3).<br>2. The recurring manual entries have run and the month's journals have been reviewed (module 2).<br>3. The standing reconciliations and checks have been performed (Chapter 2 lists them in order).<br>4. The trial balance has been scanned and its anomalies worked (module 1).<br>5. The period has been locked — the freeze date advanced so the sealed month cannot change.</p><p>A closed month is a month whose statements can be circulated without qualification. Once sealed, its figures stay as reported; corrections discovered later post in the current period under module 2's rules.</p><p><b>Why the close is monthly.</b> The alternative — trading all year and closing once — produces the year-end backlog: twelve months of unposted stragglers, unswept suspense, unreconciled banks, and unaccrued costs, all processed at once, months after the events. The monthly close avoids this by construction:</p><p>• Each month's work is small because the previous month's was done.<br>• Errors are found while their evidence is fresh and the people involved remember the transaction.<br>• The year-end becomes an ordinary close plus a short list of annual mechanics (Chapter 8).</p><p>The cost curve is predictable: a business's first monthly close takes days, because it carries the backlog; by the sixth, the close takes hours. The standing target used in this track is <b>day five</b> — the month sealed and its statements read by the fifth working day of the next month.</p><p><b>The three statements.</b> Produced from the sealed month and read together:</p><p>• The <b>profit and loss statement</b> — what the period earned and consumed (Chapter 4).<br>• The <b>balance sheet</b> — what the business owns and owes at the sealed date (Chapter 5).<br>• The <b>cash flow statement</b> — where the money went, reconciling profit to bank movement (Chapter 6).</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the close checklist. Chapter 3: cut-off. Chapters 4-6: reading the three statements. Chapter 7: fixed assets and depreciation. Chapter 8: year-end, the audit, and the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The monthly close checklist", "12", "<p>The close runs from one checklist: nine lines, each with a named owner, run in order. Order matters because later lines verify earlier ones.</p><p><b>1. Cut-off sweep.</b> The month's documents in: unsubmitted drafts found and posted (deliveries picked but unposted, receipts at the gate, invoices pending), straggler paperwork posted with honest in-period dates, and the To-Bill queues read in both directions — the unbilled delivery and the unbilled receipt both distort next month's margin. Chapter 3 covers this line in full.</p><p><b>2. The manual layer.</b> The recurring journal set run (accruals, prepayment consumptions, payroll, depreciation — module 2's templates); accrual reversals verified; the month's journal review performed (the five questions, the count, the aggregate patterns).</p><p><b>3. The sweeps.</b> Suspense to zero, each item classified to its true account. The unaddressed dimension lump to its true cost centers (module 6). Unallocated payments and unapplied credits worked (module 4's read).</p><p><b>4. The reconciliations.</b> Every bank account against its statement (module 3), remainders named and aged. Cash counts confirmed current.</p><p><b>5. The bridges.</b> Stock ledger to stock accounts, to the kobo. Tax registers to tax accounts (module 5's pre-filing check, run whether or not a filing falls this week). Control accounts to party totals (module 4's tie check).</p><p><b>6. Subsidiary outputs collected.</b> The AR/AP ritual's artifacts (aged reports, provision movement, advance schedules), the tax cycles' zero-verifications, the stock month-end's outputs — collected from their own routines, not re-performed here.</p><p><b>7. The trial balance scan.</b> Module 1's ten minutes: wrong-side balances, should-be-zero accounts with balances, unrecognised lines — each flagged and worked.</p><p><b>8. The freeze advance.</b> Stragglers confirmed in, the checks above confirmed passed, then the period locked — accounts and stock together — and the freeze month logged. The order is fixed: sweep first, freeze second. A close that freezes first manufactures its own next-month stragglers.</p><p><b>9. Statements produced and read.</b> The three statements printed from the sealed month; the management reading held (Chapters 4-6); the close bundle filed — the signed checklist plus its artifacts, one folder per month.</p><p><b>Practical rules.</b> The checklist has one owner overall and a named owner per line. Lines 1-8 target days one to four; line 9 lands day five. A line that keeps slipping is investigated as a process problem, not re-assigned as a person problem, until the cause is fixed.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Cut-off", "12", "<p>Cut-off is the rule that a period contains exactly its own documents. Cut-off errors are the close's most common defect and the P&amp;L's most direct distortion: a document in the wrong period moves real margin between months, through omission alone.</p><p><b>The completeness pairs.</b> Cut-off is checked as pairs of documents that must complete within the period:</p><p>• <b>Deliveries and their invoices.</b> A period's revenue is its invoices; its COGS is its deliveries. A month whose deliveries posted but whose invoices lagged carries COGS without revenue — margin understated this month, overstated next month when the invoices land. The <b>To-Bill (sales) queue at close is the exposure, in naira</b>: the close either bills it or carries it knowingly, with a note.<br>• <b>Receipts and their bills.</b> The Stock Received But Not Billed accrual handles this side by design: possession is booked at receipt, and the liability accrues whether or not the supplier's invoice has arrived. The close's job here is the <b>RNB age read</b>: an old RNB balance means invoices are not being processed — a workflow problem, visible as an aging accrual.<br>• <b>Consumptions and their accruals.</b> Utilities consumed but unbilled, services received but uninvoiced: covered by module 2's accrual routine, run as checklist line 2.</p><p><b>The document sweep, step by step.</b></p><p>1. List draft documents: every document type, filtered unsubmitted, dated this month. This five-minute query finds most of the stragglers.<br>2. Read the operational queues: the warehouse's unposted receipts, the billing desk's pending list.<br>3. Anticipate the known latecomers: the supplier who always bills on the 5th for the prior month is accrued by template and reversed on arrival.<br>4. Run the sweep on <b>day one</b> of the close, so stragglers post with honest in-period dates before the freeze. Sweep first, freeze second.</p><p><b>Worked example.</b> A ₦500,000 delivery posts on the 30th; its invoice posts on the 2nd. Month one records the COGS (say ₦380,000) with no revenue: margin understated by ₦120,000. Month two records ₦500,000 revenue with no COGS: margin overstated by ₦120,000. One document's timing has distorted two months' margins in opposite directions — which is why a gross margin that swings on stable trade points first to cut-off (Chapter 9, Pattern 3).</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Check the pairs: deliveries/invoices via the To-Bill queue, receipts/bills via the RNB age, consumptions via the accrual routine. Sweep on day one, with honest dates, before the freeze.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Reading the profit and loss statement", "12", "<p>The P&amp;L states what the period earned and consumed. This chapter covers its structure, the reading method, and the checks to run before trusting it.</p><p><b>Structure, top to bottom.</b></p><p>• <b>Revenue</b> — the period's invoiced trade, net of returns and credit notes.<br>• <b>Cost of goods sold</b> — the delivered goods' carried cost: the valuation releases summing.<br>• <b>Gross margin</b> — the difference, read with its <b>percentage</b>: the trading engine's health in one number, and the first line every reader checks.<br>• <b>Operating expenses</b> — the running costs, grouped as the chart's design intended them to be read.<br>• <b>Operating profit</b> — what the business earned before financing and tax.<br>• <b>Below the line</b> — finance costs; exceptional items, labelled as such (an exceptional item blended into operations distorts every trend); and the tax charge (the business's own tax — module 5's distinction) toward net profit.</p><p><b>The reading method, four rules in order.</b></p><p>1. <b>Trend before level.</b> Read this month against the last six. A level alone says little; a gross margin that moved from 24% to 21% over a quarter is a finding regardless of whether 21% is acceptable in itself.<br>2. <b>Rate before naira.</b> Margins and expense ratios compare across months of different sizes; raw naira mislead.<br>3. <b>Decompose before concluding.</b> A falling gross margin splits three ways — price (selling cheaper), mix (selling different things), or cost (paying more) — three different causes with three different owners. Module 6's dimensions do the splitting: margin by branch, by line.<br>4. <b>Drill before disputing.</b> Every line is three clicks from its documents. The disputed expense is opened live, not argued from the page.</p><p><b>The pre-reading checks.</b> Before the figures are trusted, confirm the disciplines that keep them honest held this month:</p><p>• <b>Cut-off</b> (Chapter 3) — the usual cause of a bouncing margin.<br>• <b>Valuation</b> — a COGS jump caused by a bad rate entering the valuation stream; the rate-history read is the check.<br>• <b>Accruals</b> (module 2) — the \"cheap\" month that simply has not been billed yet.<br>• <b>The dimension lump</b> (module 6) — branch reads distorted by unaddressed postings.<br>• <b>Exceptional labelling</b> — one-offs marked as one-offs.</p><p><b>The management reading.</b> Twenty minutes, monthly, on a fixed day: the three-number headline (revenue, gross margin percentage, operating profit), the trend view, the material variances with their one-line causes, and any decision the figures require. The reading is a standing meeting, not an email.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Reading the balance sheet", "12", "<p>The balance sheet states what the business owns and owes at the sealed date. The total always balances — the grammar guarantees it — so the reading discipline is line by line: every line is a claim, and every claim has a check.</p><p><b>Structure.</b> Assets: fixed assets (Chapter 7), stock, debtors, prepayments and supplier advances, WHT receivable, bank and cash. Liabilities: creditors, customer advances, accrued expenses, the tax custodies, loans. Equity: capital, retained earnings, the current year's result.</p><p><b>The line-by-line checks.</b></p><p>• <b>Stock</b> — is the figure the perpetual ledger's figure (the stock-to-accounts bridge agreeing), at honest cost, with the dead-stock and aging reads qualifying how good the asset is beyond how big?<br>• <b>Debtors</b> — gross less the provision, with the provision shown rather than hidden; tied to parties; aged honestly; advances un-netted. The check: does the aging support the provision, and does the tie hold?<br>• <b>Prepayments and supplier advances</b> — the prepaid rent consuming on schedule; the advance aging clean of items past plausible delivery.<br>• <b>WHT receivable</b> — certificated, or under active chase; the asset is only as real as its certificate file.<br>• <b>Bank and cash</b> — every balance reconciled this month, with the reconciliation statements in the close bundle; remainders young and named; floats counted.<br>• <b>Creditors</b> — tied to parties; the RNB age read; the supplier-statement reconciliations current for the top names.<br>• <b>Customer advances and accruals</b> — each with its schedule; these liabilities are what keep the P&amp;L honest.<br>• <b>Tax custodies</b> — at their explainable levels (post-remittance zero, or the stated carry-forward), with the reserve funded to match.<br>• <b>Equity</b> — retained earnings moves only by the year-end closing entry and governed distributions. An unexplained equity movement is a serious finding (Chapter 9, Pattern 5): deleted documents, forced balances, and migration errors all surface in equity, because equity is the residual where broken books hide.</p><p><b>The reading habit.</b> The balance sheet is read after the P&amp;L in the same monthly sitting: the working-capital lines against last month (stock, debtors, creditors — the movements Chapter 6 restates as cash), the custody levels, and any line whose check failed. A line that cannot answer its check is worked before the statements circulate, or circulated with an explicit note — never silently.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Reading the cash flow statement", "12", "<p>The cash flow statement reconciles two facts that regularly diverge: the P&amp;L reports a profit while the bank balance falls, or the reverse. Most trading businesses never read theirs; this chapter makes it usable.</p><p><b>Why profit and cash differ.</b> Profit records earnings: an invoice raised is revenue whether or not the customer has paid. Cash records movements. The gap between them is the balance sheet moving:</p><p>• Profit that became <b>stock</b> — margin sitting in the warehouse as goods.<br>• Profit that became <b>debtors</b> — margin sitting in unpaid invoices.<br>• Profit consumed by <b>paying creditors down</b>.<br>• Profit spent on <b>assets</b> — the cold room is a balance-sheet item, not an expense.</p><p>A profitable business can run out of cash, and a cash-rich business can be unprofitable. The cash flow statement starts from profit and shows, movement by movement, where the money went.</p><p><b>The three sections.</b></p><p>1. <b>Operating</b> — the trading engine's cash: profit, adjusted for non-cash lines (depreciation added back — it consumed no cash), and for the working-capital movements (stock up consumes cash; debtors up consumes cash; creditors up releases cash). A healthy trading business generates positive operating cash most months. A profitable business with chronically negative operating cash is growing while burying its margin in stock and debtors faster than it earns it — the figure that explains why growth \"doesn't feel like it\".<br>2. <b>Investing</b> — the asset spend: vehicles, fit-outs, equipment (Chapter 7's capitalisations landing as outflows).<br>3. <b>Financing</b> — the owners' and lenders' money: capital in, loans drawn and repaid, distributions out.</p><p>The three sections sum to the month's bank movement, which reconciles to the bank statements — the statement proving itself against module 3's reconciliation.</p><p><b>The five-minute monthly read.</b> The formal statement exists; the working read is four questions:</p><p>1. Did operating cash come in positive — did the engine generate or consume?<br>2. Which working-capital line moved most, and why (one sentence per material movement)?<br>3. What was invested, and was it planned?<br>4. What financed the difference — and is that source sustainable?</p><p>Practical rule: when the P&amp;L and the bank appear to disagree, do not argue from either — open the cash flow. The disagreement is always one of the working-capital lines, and the statement names which.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Fixed assets and depreciation", "13", "<p>Fixed assets are purchases that outlive their purchase month — vehicles, generators, cold rooms, fit-outs — and depreciation is the mechanism that spreads their cost across their useful lives. This chapter covers the layer at the level the system role requires; the policy choices belong to the accountant.</p><p><b>Capitalise or expense.</b> Every purchase invoice containing a durable item forces the decision:</p><p>• <b>Expense</b> — the cost lands in this month's P&amp;L. Correct for consumables and small tools.<br>• <b>Capitalise</b> — the cost becomes an asset, expensed gradually as depreciation. Correct for durable, material items.</p><p>The instrument is the <b>written capitalisation policy</b>: a value threshold (below it, expense regardless — a ₦40,000 printer is not worth an asset record's lifetime of administration), a category list (what the business capitalises, into which asset classes, at which useful lives — the accountant's table), and the rule that the threshold is policy, not discretion. Expensing everything to dampen a good year, or capitalising to flatter a bad one, is the same abuse as moving a provision by mood.</p><p><b>The asset register.</b> Capitalised assets live in the register — one record per asset: cost, date, class, useful life, location, and <b>cost center</b>, so depreciation lands on the branch that uses the asset. Register rules:</p><p>1. Created at purchase — the purchase invoice's capitalisation flow feeds the register; the asset is born from its document, not reconstructed from a year-end spreadsheet.<br>2. Amended as governed events.<br>3. Retired by the disposal flow — never deleted.</p><p><b>Depreciation.</b> Computed per asset from its class's method and life — straight-line is the trading business's standard — and posted monthly by the automated entry: DR depreciation expense (addressed by the asset's cost center), CR accumulated depreciation. The balance sheet carries cost less accumulated depreciation as <b>net book value</b>.</p><p><b>Disposals.</b> When an asset is sold, scrapped, or traded in, the disposal flow closes its record: proceeds against net book value, the gain or loss posted, the register entry marked disposed with its date and evidence. An asset that physically left without a disposal entry leaves the register asserting property the business no longer holds.</p><p><b>The annual verification.</b> The register is physically verified once a year — the fixed-asset equivalent of the stock count: each registered asset sighted (or its absence investigated and resolved through the disposal flow), locations and cost centers corrected, and the verification signed and filed. The balance sheet's fixed-asset line is only as reliable as the last verification.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Year-end, the audit, and the HealthTrade case", "13", "<p>Year-end is the twelfth monthly close plus a short list of annual mechanics. This chapter covers the mechanics, the external audit, and HealthTrade's close year.</p><p><b>The year-end additions.</b></p><p>1. <b>The closing entries.</b> The fiscal year's P&amp;L accounts are closed to retained earnings by the period-closing voucher — one governed entry making the year's result equity's newest layer. This is the only routine way retained earnings ever moves.<br>2. <b>Fiscal year mechanics.</b> The new year opened in the system before its first document needs it; the closing performed on the accountant's calendar. The fiscal year need not be the calendar year; the system serves either.<br>3. <b>The annual layer of every calendar</b> — the full stock count, the asset verification, the chart and settings walks, module 5's annual returns — scheduled across the season, not stacked into one week.<br>4. <b>The audit file.</b> For a monthly-closing business, the file is the year's twelve close bundles plus the annual artifacts — assembled by collation, not creation.</p><p><b>The external audit.</b> Its shape against a business that runs this track:</p><p>• <b>The interim walk-through</b> — the auditor tests the system of control: the tie, the bridges, the reconciliations, the separations. A business that passes walk-through earns sampling instead of full substantive testing, which is the audit-fee conversation in one sentence.<br>• <b>Fieldwork</b> — samples drilled to documents.<br>• <b>Confirmations</b> — module 4's circularisation, pre-answered by the year's hygiene.<br>• <b>Adjustments</b> — the auditor proposes some; the accountant agrees or contests them professionally; agreed adjustments are booked by module 2's rules — referenced entries in the current or formally reopened period, never quiet edits — so the audited statements and the ledger end the season as the same document.</p><p><b>HealthTrade's close year.</b> The first close takes four days of backlog work: the document sweep finds eleven stragglers, suspense empties (₦93,000 across six items), and the first full checklist runs to signature. Month two closes in two days; by month five the close lands on <b>day three</b> and the statements are read on day five's standing meeting. The readings produce decisions within the quarter: the cash flow read exposes the growth squeeze (positive profit, negative operating cash from stock build), which triggers the purchasing ladder review; the P&amp;L trend read catches a two-point margin slide decomposed to one branch's discounting; and the balance sheet walk retires the last unexplained line. At year-end, the audit's walk-through passes on first presentation, fieldwork samples rather than excavates, and the auditor's adjustments — three, all agreed — book by the rules. The audit closes three weeks earlier than the prior year, at a lower fee, with the statements and the ledger identical.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the seventh law", "11", "<p>The close-and-statements layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the never-closed month.</b> Symptom: periods open for a year; statement dates nobody trusts; every report a draft. Cause: the close never instituted (Chapters 1-2). Fix: the first close performed once, honestly, with its backlog; then the checklist with named owners, the day-five target, and the freeze keeping each sealed month sealed.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the year-end backlog.</b> Symptom: twelve months of accruals, reconciliations, and corrections processed in January; statements about a year nobody remembers. Cause: closing annually what should close monthly (Chapter 1) — every error twelve months stale by discovery. Fix: the monthly rhythm, which removes this pattern by construction.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the bouncing margin.</b> Symptom: gross margin swinging month to month on stable trade; owners distrusting the P&amp;L. Cause: cut-off noise read as news (Chapter 3) — the completeness pairs breaking at every boundary. Fix: the day-one document sweep, the To-Bill queues read in naira at every close, the RNB age watched, and Chapter 3's worked example taught to whoever reads the statements.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — statements produced but not read.</b> Symptom: the pages circulated and filed with no decision ever traced to them; the owner learning the business's position from the bank app. Cause: the reading habit never installed (Chapters 4-6). Fix: the twenty-minute monthly reading as a standing meeting, the reading methods of Chapters 4-6, and the three-number headline discipline.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the unexplained equity movement.</b> Symptom: retained earnings shifting between periods with no closing entry behind it; equity used as a plug for whatever would not balance. Cause: the most serious failure here — history edited at its last hiding place (Chapters 5, 8). Deleted documents, forced balances, and migration errors all surface in equity. Fix: each movement traced to its cause (the GL always records it), the closing entry restored as retained earnings' only routine mover, deletion rights removed, and the equity line added to every monthly balance-sheet walk.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Confirm you can state: the close's definition and the case for monthly closing with the day-five target; the nine checklist lines in order and why the order matters; the three completeness pairs, the day-one sweep, and the ₦500,000 worked example; the P&amp;L structure, the four reading rules, and the pre-reading checks; the balance-sheet line checks and the equity rule; the three cash flow sections and the four-question read; the capitalisation policy, the register rules, and the annual verification; and the year-end's four additions with the audit's shape.</p><p><b>The seventh law: the books close monthly, the statements are read monthly, and a sealed month stays sealed.</b> Module 8 covers the settings, authorities, and calendar that hold the whole finance layer together.</p>"),
]

