# ============================ ACCOUNTS 8 ============================
LESSONS_A8 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Accounts Settings", "11", "<p>This final module covers the structural layer that holds the finance function together: the settings, the fiscal-year and multi-company mechanics, the separation of authorities, the consolidated calendar, the handover test, the assembled control system, and the professional boundaries. It opens with the settings walk — each field a policy the system will enforce silently on every transaction.</p><p><b>The settings walk, field by field.</b> Performed with the accountant and the owner present; answers recorded in the implementation file; re-walked annually.</p><p>• <b>Frozen entries and the frozen-edit role</b> — the period lock's configuration (module 7's freeze). The frozen date's advance is part of the monthly close; the edit role is granted to two named people at most. Two health checks: a frozen date that never advances means the lock is not being used; a broadly granted edit role means the lock has no force.<br>• <b>Credit control settings</b> — the credit-limit machinery's master switches: who may override, and whether blocked documents hold or warn. The gate numbers' configured home.<br>• <b>Payment and allocation settings</b> — the automatic-allocation behaviours and the <b>unlink-on-cancel</b> setting: whether cancelling a payment silently unlinks its allocations. The wrong value here manufactures module 4's unallocated backlog on its own. For businesses trading in more than naira: the exchange gain/loss accounts wired and revaluation governed — the accountant's policy, the system's designated accounts.<br>• <b>Deferred accounting automation</b> — where the system spreads deferred revenue and expense by schedule (module 2's matching, automated): enabled deliberately, its accounts wired, its schedules reviewed like any template.<br>• <b>Precision, rounding, and the round-off account</b> — currency precision consistent everywhere (a mismatch between documents and ledgers produces kobo-level reconciliation noise permanently), and the round-off account pointed at its designated home.<br>• <b>Naming series</b> — invoices, payments, journals: chosen once with a long horizon, because the document numbers on a filed VAT return must still make sense to an auditor in year seven. Series are never restarted or reused.</p><p><b>The walk's rules.</b></p><p>1. Each field is read as the policy question it is, and the answer is written down.<br>2. Changes after go-live are governed events with a reason and a record — not toggles flipped per complaint.<br>3. The annual re-walk is a standing calendar line (Chapter 4), and it is the only routine occasion on which settings change.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Fiscal years and multiple companies", "12", "<p>Two structural mechanics of the finance layer: financial time is partitioned into fiscal years, and one system can hold several legal companies.</p><p><b>Fiscal years.</b></p><p>• <b>Created ahead.</b> The new fiscal year must exist before its first document needs it. The January 1st invoice that cannot post because nobody created the year is the most avoidable start-of-year failure; the standing practice is creating the next year at least two months early.<br>• <b>Aligned to the statutory year.</b> The calendar year for most Nigerian companies, but the system serves any twelve months; the accountant confirms.<br>• <b>Closed in sequence.</b> Module 7's closing entry per year, by the period-closing voucher.<br>• <b>The year is also the container</b> for budgets (module 6) and the axis for comparative reports — trend reads depend on clean year boundaries.<br>• <b>Backdating across a closed fiscal year</b> is the correction rules' most serious case: an entry into a closed year changes audited, filed figures. The default answer is module 2's rule — correct in the current period with remarks naming the original date. Formal reopening is the accountant's documented decision, and rare.</p><p><b>Multiple companies.</b> The system's multi-company machinery gives each company its own chart (or a shared template with per-company accounts), its own fiscal calendar, and its own separately sealed books, inside one system where group-level users can see across.</p><p><b>The rule for when a second company is right: companies follow legal reality, never reporting convenience.</b></p><p>• Separate registered entities keep separate books because the law treats them as separate persons — the audit, the tax filings, and the liability wall all run per entity.<br>• A branch is a dimension (module 6). A branch promoted to a company because someone wanted \"separate books\" acquires statutory filing obligations to solve a reporting problem a dimension solves at no cost.</p><p><b>Inter-company transactions.</b> Where entities genuinely transact — management fees, shared costs recharged, stock transferred, loans between entities — three rules apply:</p><p>1. Every inter-company transaction is documented in <b>both</b> entities: an invoice in one is a bill in the other, with mirrored references.<br>2. The inter-company balances are <b>reconciled</b> on a rhythm (Chapter 4 puts it quarterly): entity A's receivable from B equals B's payable to A, to the kobo.<br>3. Pricing and tax treatment of inter-company charges are the accountant's and tax adviser's territory — set as policy before the first charge, not improvised per invoice.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The four finance authorities", "12", "<p>The finance layer has four distinct authorities, and the control design consists of keeping them separated.</p><p><b>The four authorities.</b></p><p>1. <b>Record</b> — creating the documents and journals that write the ledger. Trading documents broadly; the journal pen narrowly (module 2's two names).<br>2. <b>Release</b> — moving actual money: the payment run's authoriser, the bank mandate's signatories (module 3's preparer-never-releaser rule).<br>3. <b>Close</b> — sealing and unsealing time: the freeze advance, the frozen-edit role, the fiscal year's closing (module 7 and Chapter 1).<br>4. <b>Configure</b> — the settings, the chart, the dimensions, the templates. The narrowest authority of all.</p><p>The design rule: <b>the authorities check each other only if they are held by different people.</b> The recorder's entries are paid by someone else's release; the releaser's payments are checked by someone else's reconciliation; the closer's seal is read by someone else's review; and nobody's routine role includes changing the configuration.</p><p><b>The pairings that must not collapse.</b> The specific separations an audit tests first:</p><p>• <b>The journal pen and the payment release in different hands.</b> A person who can write any entry and also move any money holds, in one seat, the full opportunity for fraud.<br>• <b>Payment preparer and releaser split</b> — two people, at any size (module 3).<br>• <b>The reconciler is not the sole releaser</b> — the mover of money is not its only checker.<br>• <b>Adjustment powers held narrow</b> — the write-off's two names (module 4); the journal pen's two names (module 2).<br>• <b>The frozen-edit role disjoint from the daily recorders</b> — the person posting today should not be able to repost yesterday's sealed figures.</p><p><b>Sizing.</b> At eleven people, roles share heads: the founder releases what the accountant prepares; the accountant closes what the founder's review checks. The irreducible pairs survive any size because each is two <b>people</b>, not two departments. A business too small for two people in finance is a business where the owner is one of the two — which is the standard SME arrangement, and a sound one.</p><p><b>The permission matrix.</b> The four authorities, mapped to named people, documented on one page, reviewed annually and at every personnel change — the same treatment module 3 gave the bank mandate. The review's first question is always the same: does any one person hold more than one authority without a documented reason?</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The finance calendar", "12", "<p>The finance function runs on a consolidated calendar. This chapter states it once, by frequency. Each line has a named owner; the calendar itself lives on the policy page and is reviewed annually.</p><p><b>Daily.</b></p><p>• The cash position read: balances, reconciliation recency, the day's obligations (module 3).<br>• The exceptions scan: blocked documents, failed payments, till-session variances flagged overnight.<br>• Entry hygiene at the desks: references captured, dimensions cascading — the habits that keep every later routine cheap.</p><p><b>Weekly.</b></p><p>• The bank items sweep: new statement lines, charges booked from the statement, deposits matched.<br>• The unallocated glance: module 4's read at light cadence — items caught young allocate in minutes.<br>• The payment run cycle, where the business runs one: prepared, evidenced, released by separated hands.</p><p><b>Monthly — the anchor.</b></p><p>• The close checklist (module 7's nine lines), days one to five, containing as collected inputs: the AR/AP ritual (module 4's ninety minutes), the tax cycles (module 5's six steps per custody, zeroes verified), the journal review (module 2), the bank reconciliations (module 3, every account), and the dimension lump sweep (module 6).<br>• The statements reading with management (module 7's twenty minutes) — the calendar's most important meeting.<br>• The branch P&amp;Ls issued and the budget variance read (module 6).</p><p><b>Quarterly.</b></p><p>• The provision review in depth — the matrix's judgments revisited (module 4).<br>• The correction log read by cause — upstream problems surfaced (module 2).<br>• The bank charges reasonableness read (module 3).<br>• The allocation pool audit — direct-first shrinking it (module 6).<br>• The advance agings' escalations resolved (module 4).<br>• Inter-company reconciliations, where they exist (Chapter 2).</p><p><b>Annually.</b> Scheduled across weeks, not stacked:</p><p>• The settings walk (Chapter 1) and the permission matrix review (Chapter 3).<br>• The chart walk — every account explainable in one sentence (module 1).<br>• The dimension audit (module 6) and the bank mandate review (module 3).<br>• The capitalisation policy review and the asset verification (module 7).<br>• The budget build (module 6 — November).<br>• The compliance calendar re-confirmed against current law (module 5).<br>• The year-end mechanics (module 7).</p><p>Practical rule: a calendar line without a named owner is a line that will stop running. The annual review's first task is confirming every line still has one.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The finance handover test", "12", "<p>The handover test asks one question of any function: could a competent newcomer, given only what is documented, run it for a month with zero questions that only the departing person can answer? Finance — the function most often held entirely in one trusted accountant's head — needs the test most.</p><p><b>The test, walked module by module.</b> A finance function passes when:</p><p>1. The <b>chart</b> documents the account language, every account with its one-sentence purpose (module 1).<br>2. The <b>journal governance</b> is written: the pen's two names, the recurring template set, the correction rules (module 2).<br>3. The <b>bank layer</b> is documented: every account, mode, and mandate, with last month's reconciliations showing where each remainder stands (module 3).<br>4. The <b>AR/AP ritual's</b> checklist and policy pages state the tie, the matrix, and the write-off path (module 4).<br>5. The <b>compliance calendar</b> lists every obligation, day, authority, and evidence folder — with twelve months of folders showing the pattern (module 5).<br>6. The <b>dimension design</b> is documented and the allocation policy answers every sharing question by pointing (module 6).<br>7. The <b>close checklist</b> states the month's spine in nine ordered lines, and the statement-reading method is written (module 7).<br>8. This module's <b>calendar, permission matrix, and settings record</b> hold the structure.</p><p>Every \"no\" in the walk identifies a module incompletely done. The documentation is not an extra deliverable — it is the by-product of each module done properly.</p><p><b>The absence test.</b> The practical version: the key person takes uninterrupted leave, and the function runs on its calendar. What breaks during the absence is exactly what the documentation is missing. Run deliberately, the absence test is the cheapest audit of function structure available, and its findings are corrected without crisis.</p><p><b>The role path.</b> A documented function also makes progression legible: the bookkeeper (entries and desk hygiene) grows into the accountant (the close's owner, the journal pen's holder, the statements' first reader), who grows into the controller (the calendar's owner, the policies' custodian, the management reading's co-chair). Clean closes, young remainders, and tied bridges form a promotion case written in numbers. The alternative — the function running entirely in one trusted head — is a single point of failure regardless of how competent the head is, and Chapter 9's Pattern 5 treats it as such.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Internal controls, assembled", "12", "<p>The track installed controls one module at a time. Assembled, they form a system with two families, and this chapter is the map.</p><p><b>The two families.</b></p><p>• <b>Preventive controls</b> stop an error or fraud before it posts: the separations (the four authorities, the two-names patterns), the validations (mandatory parties, mandatory dimensions, the balance rule), the fences (the forbidden journal list, frozen periods, masters-not-messages), and the gates (approval thresholds, the release separation).<br>• <b>Detective controls</b> catch what slipped through: the reconciliations (bank, supplier-statement), the ties (controls-to-parties, the bridges, the zeroing invariant), the reviews (the journal review, the trial-balance scan, the adjustments read), and the aged reads (remainder agings, the correction log, the unallocated list).</p><p><b>The pairing principle.</b> Every preventive control should have a detective partner watching its gaps: journal governance prevents, the journal review detects; the release separation prevents, the reconciliation detects; mandatory dimensions prevent, the lump sweep detects. A control map with unpaired fences has blind spots — the assembly check is walking the map and naming each control's partner.</p><p><b>The fraud triangle.</b> The standard frame: fraud requires <b>opportunity</b> (access plus an absent control), <b>pressure</b> (personal circumstances nobody sees), and <b>rationalisation</b> (the story the person tells themselves). The business controls only opportunity — which is what the preventive family shrinks: the everything-role dismantled, custody segregated, the journal pen narrowed, the masters guarded. The detective family then shortens the time to discovery: what would compound for years surfaces at the month's reconciliation. The cultural layer removes rationalisation's easiest story: controls framed as protecting the honest holder — the clean count, the signed run, the documented approval are the honest person's own evidence — are harder to resent and harder to excuse breaking.</p><p><b>The control self-assessment (CSA).</b> Annual, on evidence, one page per control: does it still run, and can it prove it ran?</p><p>1. List the controls by family.<br>2. For each, name the evidence of its last execution (the reconciliation file, the review sign-off, the approval log).<br>3. Read the tell-tale metrics: approval times (a second signature granted in seconds is not a review), remainder ages, sweep sizes.<br>4. Revive or formally retire dead controls. A designed-but-dead control is worse than an absent one, because someone is relying on it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Professional boundaries: accountant, tax adviser, auditor", "12", "<p>The finance layer is run with three professionals, and the boundaries between the system role and each of them must be held clearly. This chapter states the boundary map and the working patterns.</p><p><b>The boundary map.</b></p><p>• <b>The system role</b> — the person who configures and operates the machinery (this certification's territory) — owns the configuration, the templates, the registers, the routines, the evidence structure, and the literacy to walk any entry with any professional. It does not make policy judgments.<br>• <b>The accountant</b> owns the policy: method choices, provision matrices, capitalisation thresholds, recognition schedules, reopening decisions, and professional responsibility for the statements.<br>• <b>The tax adviser</b> owns the positions: rates, eligibility, reliefs, and stances where the law is unclear (module 5's boundary).<br>• <b>The auditor</b> owns the opinion: independent, sample-based, and best served by being served organised evidence (module 7's file).</p><p>The dividing sentence, worth memorising because it will be blurred monthly: <b>the machinery makes the professionals' judgments executable and evidenced; it does not make the judgments.</b></p><p><b>The working patterns.</b></p><p>1. <b>The accountant co-designs from day one.</b> The chart walk, the posting-map verification, and the policy pages are done with the accountant in the room. An accounts layer built without the accountant who must own it gets built twice.<br>2. <b>Habit conflicts resolve by evidence, not authority.</b> Where the system's discipline conflicts with an accountant's established habit — module 2's journal-habit case is the standard example — the resolution is demonstration and transfer, not derision.<br>3. <b>The auditor is engaged early.</b> The interim walk-through invited rather than endured, the file structure shared in advance, adjustments booked by the correction rules. The audit relationship compounds across years the same way the authority relationship does: a clean first year buys a lighter second.<br>4. <b>The tax adviser is consulted before configuration, not after.</b> The new revenue stream's VAT treatment, related-party pricing, and reform eligibility questions are settled before the templates are built — reconfiguring after a wrong position costs far more than the consultation.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Machinery, policy, positions, opinion — four owners. Hold the boundary, co-design with the accountant, meet the auditor early, and loop the tax adviser in before configuring anything the law touches.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's structural year", "13", "<p>The structural layer, installed over four quarters. Each chapter of this module appears.</p><p><b>Q1 — the settings walk (Chapters 1-2).</b> The walk runs with the accountant. Frozen entries are configured with the two-name edit role and the freeze advance is added to the close checklist. The <b>unlink-on-cancel</b> setting is found at its wrong value — it had been silently unlinking allocations whenever a payment was cancelled, which explained part of module 4's original unallocated backlog — and corrected. Naming series are confirmed for the long term. The next fiscal year is created two months early, closing off the January 1st failure before it can occur. Every answer is recorded in the implementation file.</p><p><b>Q2 — the permission matrix (Chapter 3).</b> The review finds the implementation-era all-permissions role still live on one user: the accountant, holding record, release, close, and configure \"temporarily\" since go-live. It is dismantled as a design exercise, not an accusation: the journal pen and the payment release are split (the founder releases what the accountant prepares — the two roles the business's size allows, correctly separated), the frozen-edit role is confirmed at two names, and the matrix is documented on one page. At the next audit, the walk-through passes its separation tests for the first time.</p><p><b>Q3 — the control self-assessment (Chapter 6).</b> The first CSA walks the control map by family and finds one designed-but-dead control: the second signature on large journals, live in the workflow but rubber-stamped in practice — approvals landing in seconds, unread. It is revived with an evidence habit: the approver samples the journal's references before signing, and approval times are read quarterly. The CSA also confirms the pairing principle across the map — every fence has its detective partner — and files its one-page-per-control record.</p><p><b>Q4 — the absence test (Chapter 5).</b> The founder takes three uninterrupted weeks. The trading functions had passed the test in earlier years; this year finance runs on its calendar: the close lands on day five without the founder's review, the payment runs release under the deputy-mandate design, and the compliance calendar's two in-window filings land on their days. The handover walk afterwards scores its first clean sheet — zero founder-only questions in the finance function. The MD's note in the year file: <i>the business now runs on its documents in every function, including the one that counts them.</i></p><p><b>The year in numbers.</b> Close: day five, every month of H2. Audit: walk-through passed, three agreed adjustments, fee reduced. Unallocated read: near-zero every month since the unlink fix. Permission matrix: four authorities, no undocumented overlaps. Absence test: passed.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes, the eighth law & the track's laws", "12", "<p>The structural layer's recurring failures, the module's law, and the track's eight laws assembled.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — settings changed casually.</b> Symptom: frozen dates that never advance; the unlink setting flipped per complaint; precision changed mid-year. Cause: the settings treated as toggles instead of policy (Chapter 1). Fix: settings behind the narrowest authority, changes as governed events with a record, and the annual walk as the only routine occasion for change.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the everything-role.</b> Symptom: one user holding record, release, close, and configure; the audit walk-through's first finding. Cause: setup convenience never dismantled (Chapter 3) — the full fraud opportunity assembled in one seat. Fix: the four authorities separated by the two-people patterns, the matrix documented and reviewed annually, and the dismantling run as a design exercise, not an accusation.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the branch promoted to a company.</b> Symptom: statutory filings multiplying; inter-company balances nobody reconciles; \"separate books\" that a dimension would have provided at no cost. Cause: companies created for reporting convenience instead of legal reality (Chapter 2). Fix: dimensions for reading, companies for legal persons, and the wrongly created entity wound down or made legally real with the accountant.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — designed-but-dead controls.</b> Symptom: reconciliations that quietly stopped; second signatures granted in seconds; checklist lines dropped. Cause: false assurance (Chapter 6) — worse than absence, because someone relies on the control. Fix: the annual CSA on evidence, the approval-time and remainder-age metrics, and dead controls revived or formally retired.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the single-person function.</b> Symptom: finance running entirely in one trusted head; leave never taken; a close only one person can perform; an owner who cannot read the statements unaided. Cause: competence without structure (Chapter 5) — a single point of failure regardless of skill. Fix: the handover test as the standard, the documentation as each module's by-product, the role path making structure a promotion engine, and the absence test run deliberately.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Confirm you can state: the settings walk's fields and the three walk rules; the fiscal-year rules and the companies-follow-legal-reality rule with the three inter-company disciplines; the four authorities, the pairings that must not collapse, and the sizing answer; the calendar by frequency; the handover test's eight-point walk and the absence test; the two control families, the pairing principle, the fraud triangle, and the CSA; and the four professional boundaries with the four working patterns.</p><p><b>The eighth law: structure over individuals — authorities separated, settings governed, rhythms on the calendar, and the function passes the handover test.</b></p><p><b>The track's eight laws.</b></p><p>1. The books are written by documents, into a chart designed for reading.<br>2. The journal is the exception and the document is the rule — what a document can do, a document must do.<br>3. Every bank account is reconciled monthly, and every difference is named — never plugged.<br>4. The chair keeps the record real, collectable, current and complete.<br>5. Tax money is held, not owned — reconciled, filed from the system, remitted from the reserve, and zeroed every cycle.<br>6. One chart, many lenses — every posting carries its address, contribution decides, and allocation is written.<br>7. The books close monthly, the statements are read monthly, and a sealed month stays sealed.<br>8. Structure over individuals — authorities separated, settings governed, rhythms on the calendar, and the function passes the handover test.</p><p>This completes the Accounts Professional track.</p>"),
]

